# Refactoring discipline

## Before changing code
- **Lock the goal**: one measurable outcome (e.g. "move list fetch to TanStack Query") and explicit **non-goals** (no UI redesign, no unrelated lint cleanup).
- **Prefer vertical slices**: one flow or feature path per change set; avoid repo-wide mechanical edits unless agreed.
- **Read first**: match naming, imports, folder layout, and error/toast patterns of the surrounding module.

## Behavior and risk
- **Preserve semantics** unless the task explicitly changes product behavior; call out any intentional deltas in the PR/summary.
- **Reduce blast radius**: keep public props/APIs stable; use adapters or thin wrappers at boundaries instead of renaming across many consumers in one go.
- **Validate**: run targeted checks (`tsc`, lint on touched paths, or the relevant page manually) after substantive refactors.

## Imports
- **Remove unused imports** in every touched file; keep imports ordered consistently with the surrounding file (path aliases, grouping).
- **Prefer narrow imports**: import only what you need (`import { foo } from 'bar'`), not the whole library namespace, when the library supports tree-shaking—smaller bundles and clearer dependencies.

## Data fetching, side effects, and state (this codebase)
- **TanStack Query** owns **async remote I/O and API work** (HTTP/HTTPS and any other promise-based backend calls), including reads, writes, uploads, and derived refetches. Treat it as the home for **side effects that hit an API or external service**—not only “server-side” in a narrow sense: if it goes over the network or async boundary and should be cached, deduped, retried, or invalidated, prefer **`useQuery` / `useMutation`**. Put follow-up cache work (`invalidateQueries`, `setQueryData`, `refetch`) in **`onSuccess` / `onSettled`** or dedicated small hooks—not ad‑hoc `useEffect` fetch chains when a query or mutation fits.
- Use **query keys** from `@query/keys` (maps to `src/query/keys.ts`; e.g. `communicationsKeys`, `plannerKeys`, `crmAppKeys`); follow `src/query/README.md`.
- **Redux** is for **local and global client/UI state** (modals, wizard steps, ephemeral selections, features not yet migrated). **Do not** add new Redux thunks solely for CRUD/list reads or other API I/O—use RQ. During migration, avoid duplicating the same remote list in Redux and RQ; document temporary overlap in the PR.

## Chat company / tenant react-select
- **`ChatCompanyOption`** fields (`identifier`, `tenant_id`, `name`) are optional. **Do not** inline `companies.map((c) => ({ value: c.identifier, label: c.name ?? c.identifier }))`—TypeScript will infer `string | undefined` and break react-select.
- Use **`mapChatCompaniesToSelectOptions`**, **`findChatCompanySelectOption`**, and **`resolveTenantIdFromCompany`** from `src/page-modules/chat/shared/chatCompanySelectOptions.ts` for all chat tenant/company pickers.

## Structure: components and hooks
- **Helper / presentational UI** that is reused or clutters a page belongs under **`src/components/`** with domain-first paths (e.g. `src/components/planner/...`, `src/components/communications/...`), co-located SCSS/types where the module already does.
- **Reusable stateful logic** (pagination, filters, derived data, subscription setup, modal/form orchestration, API mutations) belongs in **`src/hooks/`** (or domain `use*.ts` next to the feature when the codebase already does)—not buried inside giant page files. **Pages and section shells should mostly compose hooks + presentational JSX.**
- **Split large UI blocks**: pages should not carry multi-hundred-line JSX trees for a single feature. Extract **tab panels, modals, tables/results shells, and status bars** into co-located components (same feature folder) so each file stays readable and Sonar complexity stays down.
- **`src/pages/` is for routes only**: when splitting code out of a page, **do not** put extracted helpers, hooks-as-files, or other non-route modules in **`pages/`**. Add them under **`src/components/`** (domain-aligned path per above)—or keep them in existing **`page-modules/`** (or equivalent) feature folders—and import into the route.
- **Styles: SCSS by default; inline only when dynamic**: Put **all static** layout, spacing, typography, borders, and interactive states (`:hover`, `:focus`, `:disabled`) in **`src/assets/scss/`** (feature-scoped file, e.g. `stages-page.scss`) or the feature’s co-located stylesheet. Use **`style={{ ... }}` in TSX only** for values that **come from data at runtime** (e.g. `backgroundColor` from a user-picked hex, chart slice colors from the API). Never inject large blocks of static CSS via `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`—add a real SCSS module/file instead.
- **Inline style types**: Objects passed to `style={...}` must satisfy `React.CSSProperties`—annotate the object (`const x: React.CSSProperties = { ... }`), use `satisfies React.CSSProperties`, or add `as const` on narrowed literals (`overflowX`, `display`, `position`, etc.). See `react-inline-styles.mdc`.

## Memory leaks and rerenders
- **Subscriptions**: always return cleanup from `useEffect` (listeners, timers, `EventSource`/SSE wrappers, observers). Avoid orphaned globals.
- **Stable references**: memoize callbacks/values passed to memoized children when identity churn causes extra renders; fix **dependency arrays**—no stale closures, no missing deps that hide bugs.
- **Lists**: stable `key`s; avoid inline object/array literals for props on heavy child trees in hot paths unless measured harmless.

## Code quality during refactors
- **No drive-by changes**: do not reformat, rename, or "clean up" files you are not touching for the refactor goal.
- **Extract before duplicating**: shared fetch/transform logic belongs in one module (e.g. `fetchXPayload` + thin `useQuery`), not copied between thunk and component.
- **Sonar-friendly**: avoid huge parameter lists in public APIs—use a single `params` object (query keys, hook options). Keep complexity down with small helpers.

## Recurring Sonar pitfalls to NEVER introduce
These warnings keep reappearing across refactors. Apply the right pattern up-front so the next agent does not have to clean them up again.

### Promises and the `void` operator
- **NEVER prefix a promise with `void`** to silence the floating-promise lint. Sonar flags `void promise()` as a code smell.
- **DO** chain `.catch(() => undefined)` when you genuinely want fire-and-forget behaviour, or `await` the call when inside an `async` function.
- **TanStack Query invalidation** is the most common offender. Use:
  ```ts
  queryClient
    .invalidateQueries({ queryKey: someKeys.all() })
    .catch(() => undefined);
  ```
  not `void queryClient.invalidateQueries(...)`.

### Type assertions
- **NEVER add `as SomeType` or `as unknown as SomeType` when the receiver already accepts the source type.** Sonar will flag this on the next scan ("This assertion is unnecessary…").
- **NEVER use the non-null assertion `value!` as a shortcut to silence TS** when `value` may be `null | undefined`. Prefer:
  - narrowing inside the function body (`if (value === null) return Promise.reject(...)`), or
  - changing the upstream type so the value can never be nullable at the call site.
- For TanStack `useQuery` with `enabled: id !== null`, build the `queryFn` so TS narrows correctly:
  ```ts
  queryFn: () => {
    if (id === null) return Promise.reject(new Error("ID required"));
    return getById(id);
  },
  enabled: id !== null,
  ```

### Negated conditions in ternaries / `if/else`
- Sonar's "Unexpected negated condition" rule fires on patterns like `cond != null ? a : b` or `if (!cond) {...} else {...}`. Flip them to the positive form:
  - `cond == null ? b : a` or `cond === null ? b : a`
  - `if (cond) {...} else {...}`

### `Array#sort()` without a compare function
- Always pass an explicit comparator. For strings use `localeCompare`:
  ```ts
  arr.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b));   // strings
  arr.sort((a, b) => a - b);                // numbers
  ```
- Even when "the items are obviously strings", Sonar still flags bare `.sort()`.

### Default object stringification (`[object Object]`)
- Never put a value of type `unknown` (or anything that may be an object) directly into a template literal or call `String(value)` on it. Both produce `"[object Object]"` when the value is a record.
- Add a small helper (or reuse one) when you need to stringify an id-like field:
  ```ts
  function toSafeIdString(v: unknown): string {
    if (typeof v === "string") return v;
    if (typeof v === "number" || typeof v === "bigint") return v.toString();
    return "";
  }
  ```

### Nested ternaries
- Extract any `a ? b : c ? d : e` into a named helper or `useMemo`. Even two-level nesting trips Sonar.

### `replace` vs `replaceAll`
- Prefer `string.replaceAll("_", " ")` over `string.replace(/_/g, " ")`. Sonar prefers the explicit `replaceAll` API.

### Skip-key arrays
- When you have a list of "keys to ignore", make it a `Set<string>` at module scope and use `.has(key)`, not `includes`.

### Cognitive complexity caps
- Aim to keep functions (especially top-level React components and data-fetching helpers) **at or below 15**. If you find yourself adding a fourth `if/else` branch or a third nested loop, **extract a helper**.
- For React pages, hoist branches into small sub-components (`<TasksSection/>`, `<HeaderActions/>`, etc.) and pull `useQuery` orchestration into a `useXyzPage()` hook in `src/hooks/`.

### CSS
- Don't declare the same property twice in a single rule block (e.g. two `font-family` lines). The stylelint/Sonar rule will flag it.

### Regular expressions and ReDoS
Sonar's `typescript:S5852` rule ("regex which is vulnerable to super-linear runtime due to backtracking") is one of the most common regressions in this codebase. **Avoid hand-rolled regex** for anything that can be expressed with string operations, and when you genuinely need a regex follow the guards below.

- **NEVER write regexes with overlapping `*`/`+` quantifiers around the same character class.** Patterns like:
  ```ts
  s.replace(/\s*\([^)]*\)\s*$/, "")   // ReDoS-prone: \s* on both sides + [^)]* in the middle
  s.match(/^(\w+)+$/)                  // classic catastrophic backtracking
  s.match(/(a|aa|aaa)*b/)              // alternation with overlap
  ```
  are flagged by Sonar (and can hang the thread on adversarial input).
- **DO prefer non-regex string operations** when the intent is simple:
  - "Strip the trailing `"(...)"` suffix from a display name" → use `stripTrailingParenthetical` from `@utils/displayName` (linear, uses `lastIndexOf("(")` + `slice`).
  - "Replace all underscores with spaces" → `value.replaceAll("_", " ")`, not `value.replace(/_/g, " ")`.
  - "Remove a known prefix" → `value.startsWith(p) ? value.slice(p.length) : value`.
- **When a regex is genuinely required**, keep it boring and linear:
  - Use **specific** character classes (`[^()]` instead of `[^)]`) so the engine can't wander.
  - Anchor on both sides (`^…$`) and prefer **non-overlapping** segments.
  - Avoid `\s*` on both sides of a quantified group; use a single `\s*` plus a more specific inner pattern.
  - Cap input length before applying the regex on user-supplied strings.
- **Reach for the shared helper before adding a regex**: `src/utils/displayName.ts` already exists. If you find yourself writing a regex to clean a display name / agent label / "Name (ext 123)" pattern, import `stripTrailingParenthetical` instead. Add new helpers there (or in `src/utils/`) when you have a new string-cleaning need that two or more call sites share.

## PR / handoff
- Describe **what**, **why**, and **how to verify** in complete sentences; link or name tickets when applicable.
